auth {
  mode: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
  MCP-Protocol-Version: {{protocolVersion}}
}

script:post-response {
  // Streamable HTTP answers a JSON-RPC request with a *Server-Sent Event*, not JSON:
  //
  //     event: message
  //     data: {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{...}}
  //
  // Bruno hands that to us as a raw string, so unwrap the `data:` lines once here and
  // every test below can read `res.getBody().result` like any other JSON API.
  const raw = res.getBody();
  if (typeof raw === "string" && raw.indexOf("data:") !== -1) {
    const payload = raw
      .split(/\r?\n/)
      .filter(line => line.startsWith("data:"))
      .map(line => line.slice(5).trim())
      .join("");
    if (payload) {
      res.setBody(JSON.parse(payload));
    }
  }
}

tests {
  // Runs after EVERY request in this collection.
  test("the answer is a JSON-RPC 2.0 envelope", function() {
    const body = res.getBody();
    if (body && typeof body === "object") {      // 202 Accepted has no body at all
      expect(body.jsonrpc).to.equal("2.0");
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # HR Skills MCP Server

  The same HR domain as [`fast-api/`](../../fast-api/README.md), published as **MCP tools**
  instead of REST endpoints. Start the server first:

      cd mcp-server
      uv run app/main.py          # http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp

  Then pick the **Local** environment (top right) and run the collection **top to bottom**.

  > If port 8000 is taken — most likely by `fast-api/` — start this one with
  > `uv run fastmcp run app/main.py --transport http --port 8010` and change `baseUrl`.

  ## Why this collection looks so unlike a REST one

  Every request here is a `POST` to the **same URL**, `{{baseUrl}}/mcp`. There are no paths,
  no verbs and no resources; the method name lives in the JSON body:

      {"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 1, "method": "tools/call",
       "params": {"name": "shortlist", "arguments": {"job_id": "J2001"}}}

  Three things follow from that, and each one has a request in the **Errors** folder proving
  it:

  1. **The status code stops carrying meaning.** A tool that fails answers `200` with
     `isError: true` in the body. `4xx` is reserved for the *transport* being used wrong.
  2. **Responses arrive as Server-Sent Events**, not JSON — see the post-response script
     above, which unwraps them so the tests can stay readable.
  3. **The connection is stateful.** `initialize` mints a session id; every later request
     must echo it back in `mcp-session-id` or the server answers `404`.

  ## Order matters more than usual

  **Handshake** must run first. It captures `mcpSessionId` into a runtime variable that every
  other request sends as a header, so running *Tools › Shortlist the best candidates* on its
  own in a fresh Bruno session gets a `404` — the literal text `{{mcpSessionId}}` is not a
  session. That is not a quirk of Bruno; it is the protocol. Run the folder, or the whole
  collection, and it resolves itself.

  Nothing here mutates server state, so the collection is re-runnable as often as you like.
}
